### Findings

#### 1. `string.Split` + LINQ allocations in `FixedNumberOfWordsTruncator` (3 instances) 🔴 Critical

**Impact:** Every call allocates a `string[]`, LINQ iterator objects, and a final joined string — O(n) GC pressure per invocation on every displayed string.

**Files:** `Truncation.cs:L178, L187, L190`

**Fix:** Use `MemoryExtensions.Split` (.NET 9+) or a span-based word counter to avoid the array, and use `string.Concat`/`AsSpan` slicing to build the result:

```csharp
public string? Truncate(string? value, int length, string? truncationString, TruncateFrom truncateFrom = TruncateFrom.Right)
{
    if (value is null) return null;
    if (value.Length == 0) return value;

    truncationString ??= string.Empty;

    // Count words and find split points without allocating
    ReadOnlySpan<char> span = value.AsSpan();
    int wordCount = 0;
    int lastWordEnd = 0;
    int firstWordStart = -1;
    int targetWordEnd = -1;
    int targetWordStart = -1;

    int i = 0;
    while (i < span.Length)
    {
        // Skip spaces
        while (i < span.Length && span[i] == ' ') i++;
        if (i >= span.Length) break;

        int wordStart = i;
        // Find end of word
        while (i < span.Length && span[i] != ' ') i++;

        wordCount++;
        if (wordCount == 1) firstWordStart = wordStart;
        if (wordCount == length)
            targetWordEnd = i;  // end index of the Nth word from start

        lastWordEnd = i;
    }

    if (wordCount <= length) return value;

    if (truncateFrom == TruncateFrom.Right)
    {
        return StringReadabilityExtensions.Concat(
            span[..targetWordEnd],
            truncationString.AsSpan());
    }

    // Find the start of the last `length` words
    int remaining = length;
    for (int j = span.Length - 1; j >= 0; j--)
    {
        if (j == 0 || (span[j] != ' ' && span[j - 1] == ' '))
        {
            remaining--;
            if (remaining == 0)
            {
                return StringReadabilityExtensions.Concat(
                    truncationString.AsSpan(),
                    span[j..]);
            }
        }
    }

    return value;
}
```

---

#### 2. Double allocation in `FixedLengthTruncator` right-truncation (1 instance) 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** `value[..n].TrimEnd()` allocates a substring, then `TrimEnd()` may allocate another, then `+ truncationString` allocates a third. Up to 3 allocations per call.

**Files:** `Truncation.cs:L72`

**Fix:** Use span-based trimming to find the correct endpoint, then a single `string.Concat`:

```csharp
// Replace line 72 with:
ReadOnlySpan<char> slice = value.AsSpan(0, length - truncationString.Length).TrimEnd();
return StringReadabilityExtensions.Concat(slice, truncationString.AsSpan());
```

